sections.body:
Debt capacity is the **lowest** amount allowed by several constraints:
- a **leverage** limit that lenders and rating agencies accept (x times EBITDA);
- a **minimum interest cover** (EBITDA / interest);
- the ability to repay a meaningful share of the debt from FCF within the life of the loans.

**Debt capacity = min (leverage limit x EBITDA, EBITDA / (minimum cover x interest rate))**

This is why rates matter so much for LBOs. When rates rise, the cover constraint binds, the company can borrow fewer turns of EBITDA, the sponsor must write a larger equity cheque and the IRR falls, unless the entry price falls too. It is the mechanism behind lower buyout volumes and wider bid-ask gaps between buyers and sellers when rates go up.
